now we have  robert cruz  who's going to talk about an analysis  of a provider survey which many here in  this room participated in  looking at prevention services for men  of sex with men  okay  thank you leaf  good morning everyone as chris uh just  mentioned last fall i was charged by the  department of public health to conduct a  provider survey survey on hiv sti  services for msm in connecticut  the department of public health aids and  chronic disease section initiative is to  respond to the significant higher  incidence  of hiv sti infections among men who have  sex with men as well as significant  numbers of co-infections and to respond  to the need for more prevention  interventions directly targeting msm  the goals of this assessment were to  conduct an analysis among providers  focusing on perceived prevention needs  barriers successes challenges and  perceptions of capacity related to  providing hiv prevention services to msm  in connecticut and to identify capacity  building assistance and training needs  of providers to help ensure  more hiv prevention services for msm in  connecticut in the future  the design of the survey and analysis  was as follows  interviews were held with 16 providers  across the state of connecticut  who provided their professional opinions  about msm hiv sti prevention topics  16 providers across the state of  connecticut  13 of these were dph funded hiv  prevention contractors  two were health care providers that  specialize in hiv care  and one was an academic research  institution  frequency analysis were conducted  there were 19 open-ended questions  the top three answers were reported most  of the questions allowed for multiple  answers and some of the questions were  designed for agencies providers or  contractors and not applicable for a  physician or a research entity  so here are some of the analysis  findings in respect to hiv sdi  prevention needs for msm the majority of  respondents indicated that young msm  population should be the top priority  followed by risk reduction and more  awareness of stis  differences in ethnic and cultural needs  clients fear of rejection  stigma and no one message fits all msm  were reported the most as barriers to  providing msm disease prevention  services  when it comes to interventions  considered as not being successful  poorly crafted message  one-size-fits-all approach and  limitations around the design of the  interventions were considered the most  significant  inadequate approaches by respondents  lack of lgbt community leadership  poor outreach job done locally and the  difficulty of reaching the msn community  where the top response is considered to  be challenges of providing hiv sti  prevention services to msm  in the area of having the ideal capacity  for the implementation of execution of  hiv sti prevention services for msm  respondents indicated that funds was a  major factor recruitment of volunteers  and the addition of more staff  staff as their top needs  in regards to increasing the capacity  at a statewide level responders  considered that a mass campaign  workshops among providers and the state  working closer with providers were the  most important areas to be targeted  as far as the hiv sta prevention  services that respondents would like to  offer to the msm in their respective  communities community outreach risk  reduction and basic outreach were the  top three answers  trends experienced by msm in connecticut  reported as being priorities by  providers where the risk of using  internet websites for hookups followed  by the risk associated with the use of  recreational drugs at parties and a  young msm sti diagnosis pattern as we  heard earlier  it was recommended by providers the need  of a change in perceptions by the msn  community reporting as equally important  in three areas increasing the  self-esteem of msm  that sdi can happen to anyone and  educating msm about safe sex being an  option  this assessment concluded a need for a  more cohesive approach where agencies  around the state come together and  exchange ideas a need for capacity  building assistance training for  providers on the issues related to  providing hiv sti prevention services to  msm  a need for new strategies to reach msm  with prevention messages including the  use of technological innovations such as  the internet and cell phone applications  and the need to address cultural racial  ethnic and social challenges of  providing hiv sti prevention services to  msm  this assessment contributes  significantly to understanding the msm  gear hiv sti prevention services  barriers and needs in the state of  connecticut and provides a foundation  for future recommendations of effective  interventions for msm in connecticut  muchas gracias

